          %h2#definition Definition
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              A
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              is called
              %em extremally disconnected
              if the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/closure"} closure
              of any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subset"} open subset
              is still an open subset, or, equivalently, the closures of disjoint open subsets are disjoint.
          %h2#properties Properties
          %p
            The extremally disconnected spaces can be characterized among the Hausdorff spaces by the fact that any continuous function mapping a dense subspace of an extremally disconnected space to a compact Hausdorff space, can be extended to the whole space. They can also be characterized among the completely regular spaces by the fact that the lattice of continuous functions mapping an extremally dis connected space to the unit interval is complete (cf. (
            %a{:href => "#GilimanJerison1960"}> Giliman-Jerison, 1960
            ), 3N and 6M). (
            %a{:href => "#Strauss67"}> Strauss 1967
            )
          %h3#as_projective_objects As projective objects
          %p
          #GleasonTheorem.num_theorem
            %h6 Theorem
            %p
              %strong
                (
                %a{:href => "#Gleason58"}> Gleason 1958
                )
              %br/
              Extremally disconnected topological spaces are precisely the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+objects"} projective objects
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
              of
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+topological+space"} compact
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hausdorff+topological+spaces"} Hausdorff topological spaces
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+spaces"} topological spaces
              and
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+maps"} proper maps
              between them.
          %p
            See e.g. (
            %a{:href => "BhattScholze13"}> Bhatt-Scholze 13, below theorem 1.8
            ). For the second part see (
            %a{:href => "#Strauss67"}> Strauss 1967
            ).
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
              a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/w-contractible+ring"} w-contractible ring
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+set"} profinite set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Spec
                    %mi R
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \pi_0(Spec R)
              is an extremally disconnected
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+set"} profinite set
          %p
            Part of (
            %a{:href => "#BhattScholze13"}> Bhatt-Scholze 13, theorem 1.8
            ).
          %h3#in_terms_of_logic In terms of logic
          %p
          .num_remark
            %h6 Remark
            %p
              The extremal disconnectedness of a space is correlated with the property that the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/frame+of+opens"} frame of its open subsets
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/De+Morgan+Heyting+algebra"} De Morgan Heyting algebra
              hence with the validity of the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/De+Morgan+law"} De Morgan law
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/logic"} logic
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topos"} topos
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Sh
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Sh(X)
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+sheaves"} of sheaves
              on a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/De+Morgan+topos"} De Morgan topos
              precisely when
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is extremally disconnected and this implies that all
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/subobject+lattices"} subobject lattices
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sub(X)
              are De Morgan Heyting algebras, in particular
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi sub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} sub(1)
              corresponding to the frame of opens of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              (cf.
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/De+Morgan+topos"} De Morgan topos
              for details and references).
          %h3#AsALiftingProperty As a lifting property
          %p
            Being extremally disconnected is equivalently a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lifting+property"} lifting property
            with respect to a surjective proper morphism of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+topological+spaces"} finite topological spaces
            (for more see the discussion at
            = succeed "):" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axioms+in+terms+of+lifting+properties"} separation axioms in terms of lifting properties
          %p
            Namely that a topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is extremally disconnected means equivalently that it solves the following lifting problems (from
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axioms+in+terms+of+lifting+properties#IllustrationOfLiftingForExtremallyDisconnected"} here
