          %p
            Here boxes indicate
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
            in a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+topological+space"} finite topological space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo →
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x\to c
            means that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi c
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} c
            is in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+closure"} closure
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %em
              %a{:href => "separation+axioms+in+terms+of+lifting+properties#BackgroundAndNotation"} Background and notation
            for more.
          %p
            One may deduce Gleason’s theorem (Prop.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#GleasonTheorem"}
          %p
            Both being surjective and being proper are right lifting properties. Hence, the existence of a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/weak+factorization+system"} weak factorization system
            generated by this morphism implies that each space admits a surjective proper map from an extremally disconnected space.
          %p
            For more see at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/separation+axioms+in+terms+of+lifting+properties"} separation axioms in terms of lifting properties
            the section
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "separation+axioms+in+terms+of+lifting+properties#ExtremallyDisconnectedSpacesBeingProjective"} extremally disconnected spaces being projective
          %h2#examples Examples
          .num_example
            %h6#example Example
            %p
              For
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              any
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set"} set
              regarded as a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topological+space"} discrete topological space
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone-Cech+compactification"} Stone-Cech compactification
              is extremally disconnected.
          %p
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BhattScholze13"} Bhatt-Scholze 13, example 2.4.6
          .num_example
            %h6#counterexample Counter-Example
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/profinite+set"} profinite set
              underlying the
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-adic+integers"} p-adic integers
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+space"} Stone space
              %em not
              extremally disconnected.
          %p
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BhattScholze13"} Bhatt-Scholze 13, example 2.4.7
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/totally+disconnected+space"} totally disconnected space
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/De+Morgan+topos"} De Morgan topos
          %h2#references References
          %p The result on projective spaces stems from
          %ul
            %li#Gleason58
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+M.+Gleason"}> Andrew M. Gleason
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Projective topological spaces
                %strong 2
                no.4A (1958) pp.482-489. (
                %a{:href => "https://projecteuclid.org/euclid.ijm/1255454110"}> euclid:ijm/1255454110
                )
            %li#Strauss67
              %p
                D. P. Strauss, (1967).
                %em Extremally Disconnected Spaces.
                Proceedings of the American Mathematical Society, 18(2), 305.
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.2307/2035286"} doi:10.2307/2035286
          %p Textbook account:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Johnstone"}> Peter Johnstone
                ,
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Stone+Spaces"} Stone Spaces
            %li#GilimanJerison1960
              %p L.Giliman, M.Jerison. Rings of continuous functions. Van Nostrand, 1960.
          %p
            Discussion in the context of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pro-etale+site"} pro-etale site
          %ul
            %li#BhattScholze13
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bhargav+Bhatt"}> Bhargav Bhatt
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Peter+Scholze"} Peter Scholze
              %em The pro-étale topology for schemes
              (
              %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1309.1198"}> arXiv:1309.1198
              )
